< Ad platforms and marketplaces show bidders a bid landscape, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice to them or to their agents, which increasingly run on reasoning language models. We ask where the value of the reasoning tokens goes in that decision. Six small ($\le$14B) open models choose among 5 or 7 bids with empirical win curves from eBay prices, under forced budgets of up to 768 tokens, and are scored on held-out auctions against an exact calculator. They recover 42--85\% of the calculator's lead; arithmetic is a fifth of the remaining loss for one model and at most 5\% for the others. The rest has two sources. For the Qwen models, 73--79\% of it is the budget: the list of options is unfinished when the answer is due, and when the same greedy trace finishes its list by 768 tokens the answer is right in 84--88\% of cases. Phi-mini and Granite-8B finish the list and then answer against it: even without forcing, 40--50\% of their finished traces end on a bid other than the one the trace ranked best, following list position within a scenario, a pattern that replicates on synthetic auctions. Four models' wrong answers favour the highest bid on the menu, about twice as often as chance; this costs bidders under pay-your-bid pricing but not under eBay's own rule. Sending answers that disagree with the trace's own numbers to a calculator recovers 73--93\% of the gap on products not used to set the rule and 82--100\% on synthetic traces.
