< We compare each wrong answer with the non-optimal options on the same menu. DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models take the highest bid, which is also the most likely to win, in 40--48\% of wrong answers against 18--19\% by chance, and these choices carry 63--78\% of the loss from missing a written best. Phi-mini takes the first-listed option in 43\% (chance 18\%) and almost never the last (1\%); Granite-8B takes the runner-up in 47\% (chance 20\%) and almost never the first-listed option (3\%). Wrong answers repeat the model's budget-zero answer under the same option order in 28--62\% of cases, against 17--46\% for the budget-zero answer under another order of the same menu and 17--38\% for a baseline that keeps the model's own preference for high or low bids; for Phi-mini the excess appears only under the same order (45\% vs 17\% and 17\%), so it is positional. With scenario fixed effects over the three option orders, which vary the best option's position but not its difficulty, moving the best option from first to last multiplies the odds of passing over it by 12 [7, 21] for Phi-mini (369 events), 3.4 [1.3, 8.8], 35 [6, 197] and 14 [3, 54] for Qwen3-4B, 8B and 14B (52--85 events) and 0.06 [0.03, 0.13] for Granite-8B (221), with no clear effect for DeepSeek-8B (0.67 [0.28, 1.6]): position affects four models after a Bonferroni correction over six (Qwen3-4B's effect does not survive), in opposite directions. On the synthetic auctions Phi-mini (17 [6, 48]) and Granite-8B (0.09 [0.02, 0.39]) repeat the pattern; the others have too few events.
