> Wrong answers are not random. DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models favour the highest bid on the menu, the one most likely to win, at about twice the chance rate, and these choices carry most of their loss (Table~\ref{tab:checks}, High bid). Phi-mini favours the first-listed option and Granite-8B the runner-up. List position has an effect within a scenario: with scenario fixed effects over the three option orders, moving the best option to the end of the list makes Phi-mini and the larger Qwen models more likely to pass over it and Granite-8B less likely (Position); Qwen3-4B's effect does not survive a Bonferroni correction and DeepSeek-8B shows none. The Phi-mini and Granite-8B patterns replicate on the synthetic auctions. Wrong answers also repeat the model's own budget-zero answer more often than baselines that keep its preference for high or low bids.
