< Per 1{,}000 decisions at 768 tokens, against the calculator's choices, pay-your-bid pricing on the empirical curve raises sellers' expected revenue by \$18k--\$83k and lowers bidders' surplus by \$15k--\$36k, mostly on Cartier; on the order-statistic curve the figures are \$16k--\$62k and \$5k--\$14k. The tilt toward high bids is a fact about the menu, but whether it is overbidding depends on the curve: 81--91\% of the wrong answers of DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models bid above the calculator on the empirical curve (55--61\% by chance), but only 38--57\% of all models' wrong answers bid above the optimum on the order-statistic curve and 47--66\% on the first-price equilibrium curve. Under eBay's own rule, where the winner pays about the closing price, higher bids are weakly better, as theory predicts: the same choices raise both seller revenue (\$5k--\$26k) and bidder surplus (\$8k--\$21k for five models), and five models' budget-zero answers score above the calculator's choice (0.91--0.98 vs 0.89). So the cost of the errors is a property of pay-your-bid pricing, and the dollar figures here are pay-your-bid figures.
---
> Whether the pull toward high bids costs money depends on the pricing rule. Under pay-your-bid pricing it does: per 1{,}000 decisions it shifts tens of thousands of dollars of expected revenue to sellers and lowers bidders' surplus by \$15k--\$36k, mostly on Cartier. Under eBay's own rule, where the winner pays about the closing price, higher bids are weakly better, as theory predicts, and the same choices raise both seller revenue and bidder surplus. The dollar figures in this paper are therefore pay-your-bid figures.
